{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791636252,"nonce":"/5+8Si8IVRHNOKeeP7EqixzUzjY=","title":"SSA construction is quadratic in large bodies: predecessor queries rescan every block, dominator intersections rebuild the RPO map","message":"Two lookups in the SSA builder cost the whole function each time they are asked:\n\n- current_preds_of, before the predecessor lists are exact, walked every block's terminator to find the blocks branching to one block, and a variable read calls it at every step of its walk up single-predecessor chains (and cloned the stored list into a fresh Vec each time). A Lua chunk that uses the debug library carries an upvalue accessor with one arm per captured upvalue; calls.lua loads a chunk capturing 200 locals, whose accessor is 4,219 blocks and took 1.8 s to lower (89% of it in current_preds_of; 50, 100, 200 upvalues took 16, 102, 687 ms).\n- DominanceInfo::intersect rebuilt the reverse-postorder position map, a SipHash IndexMap over every block, on each call, and it is called for every predecessor of every block on every iteration. errors.lua loads a chunk of 1,000 global assignments (13,018 blocks) four times; each took about 460 ms to lower (84% in that map; 250, 500, 1000 statements took 33, 124, 462 ms).\n\nFix: an index from each block to the blocks whose terminator branches to it (branches, conditional branches, switches: the edges current_preds_of counts), built on first use and kept current from a list of blocks whose terminators were written since; every terminator write in the builder records its block, and the lambda swap rebuilds it. Hits are returned in the function's block order, as the scan returned them, and debug builds compare every answer against the full scan. Walks read the stored list in place once edges are exact, and current_preds_of returns a SmallVec. The RPO positions are built once per dominance computation; idom and the frontiers are Fx maps.\n\nMeasured (Mac, release, llvm-backend build): the 200-upvalue accessor 560 -\u003e 42 ms; the 1,000-statement chunk 462 -\u003e 38 ms and now linear. Total lower_program time per official file: calls.lua 3,658 -\u003e 169 ms, errors.lua 2,354 -\u003e 359, nextvar 489 -\u003e 217, coroutine 340 -\u003e 173, locals (1,285 loads) 1,474 -\u003e 1,017, api 362 -\u003e 354. Interleaved whole-run wall, 3 runs: calls.lua 5.30 -\u003e 2.07 s, errors.lua 5.33 -\u003e 2.03 s, locals 7.28 -\u003e 5.98 s, api 698 -\u003e 615 ms, coroutine 894 -\u003e 786 ms; peak footprint unchanged within noise. Start-up instructions (PROFILE_STARTUP=10, ZYNTAX_DISABLE_WARM_UP=1, median of 3): 17 Lua benchmarks -3.7% (method_call -7.7%, nbody -6.1%), 8 Python kernels -4.5% (scimark_fft -9.0%), outputs unchanged. Optimised, lowered and interpreter bodies identical after id masking on 8 official files (2,541 bodies) and all 17 benchmarks.","files":null}]}